.\"	$OpenBSD$
.\"
.Dd $Mdocdate: April 22 2026 $
.Dt RESIZE_FFS 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm resize_ffs
.Nd resize an FFS file system with journaled recovery support
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Cm --offset Ar bytes
.Op Cm --size Ar bytes
.Op Cm --policy Ar truncate | shrink-move | push-forward
.Op Cm --shrink-side Ar end | beginning
.Op Cm --journal Ar path
.Op Cm --moves Ar path
.Op Cm --dry-run
.Op Cm --recover
.Op Cm --revert
.Op Cm --resume
.Op Cm --force
.Op Cm --progress
.Op Cm --check-invariants
.Op Cm --verbose
.Ar target
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility resizes an existing
.Xr ffs 7
file system stored in
.Ar target .
It can grow a file system, shrink it by relocating live data, and maintain a
journal that supports replay, resume, and revert operations if the resize is
interrupted.
.Pp
The following options are available:
.Bl -tag -width "--check-invariants"
.It Cm --offset Ar bytes
Specify the offset of the file system inside
.Ar target .
The default is zero.
.It Cm --size Ar bytes
Resize the file system to
.Ar bytes ,
which must be a multiple of the fragment size.
If omitted, the size is inferred from the containing partition or file,
rounded down to a multiple of the fragment size.
As with
.Xr growfs 8 ,
if the last cylinder group would be too small to hold its superblock copy,
cylinder group block, inode table and one data block, it is not created and
the size is truncated to a whole number of cylinder groups; a warning gives
the number of sectors that cannot be allocated.
This rule applies to new cylinder groups and to shrinking; when the size is
not reduced, the existing last cylinder group is kept as long as it satisfies
.Xr newfs 8 Ns 's
own rule (room for its metadata, rounded up to a block), so the file system
is never made smaller unless a smaller size is asked for.
If the resulting size equals the current size and
.Cm --size
was omitted or equals the current size,
.Nm
prints
.Dq nothing to do ,
does not modify
.Ar target
and exits 0; an explicit larger
.Cm --size
that gains nothing fails with
.Dq not enough new space .
The new number of cylinder groups must fit in the existing cylinder group
summary area; growing beyond that is refused.
.It Cm --policy Ar truncate | shrink-move | push-forward
Select the resize policy.
.Cm truncate
is the default.
.Cm shrink-move
shrinks the file system and shifts it upward inside the containing image.
.Cm push-forward
is reserved for future use and is currently only accepted for non-shrinking
operations.
.It Cm --shrink-side Ar end | beginning
Compatibility alias for selecting the shrink policy.
.It Cm --journal Ar path
Store journal state in
.Ar path .
The default is
.Pa ./resize.journal .
.It Cm --moves Ar path
Load an explicit relocation plan from
.Ar path
instead of generating one automatically.
Each line holds five numbers: old block, new block (both in fragments),
size in bytes, absolute byte offset of the pointer to the block, and the
pointer width (4 or 8).
The lines are executed in file order, except that all lines for one block
are done together, at the position of its first line.
A pointer that lies in a block moved by an earlier line is updated in the
new copy of that block.
A plan is refused before anything is written if a fragment run crosses a
block boundary, a destination lies beyond the new size, any source or
destination overlaps another, a source is not allocated or a destination
not free in its cylinder group, a pointer does not hold its old block or
has the wrong width, or an allocated fragment beyond the new size is not
moved by any line.
.It Cm --dry-run
Print the planned resize operation without modifying the target.
.It Cm --recover
Replay any unapplied journal records and stop.
.It Cm --revert
Undo the journaled changes in reverse order, including a step that was
interrupted, and reset the journal.
This also undoes moving inodes out of removed cylinder groups.
.It Cm --resume
Replay pending journal records and finish a partially completed resize.
A shrink interrupted while moving inodes or blocks is finished by planning
the blocks still beyond the new size again from the file system.
.It Cm --force
Accepted for compatibility.
A shrink that does not fit (not enough free inodes or free space below the
new size) or would leave allocated data beyond the new size is refused
even with
.Cm --force ,
before anything is written.
.It Cm --progress
Display progress while relocation or shift work is running.
.It Cm --check-invariants
Run additional on-disk consistency checks after each mutating phase:
geometry, cylinder group summaries, that used FFS2 inodes lie below
.Va cg_initediblk ,
and that every block a used inode refers to lies inside the file system,
is allocated, and is not shared with metadata or another reference.
.It Cm --verbose
Print additional progress and planning information.
.El
.Pp
Before resizing a file system inside a disk partition, adjust the partition
geometry first with
.Xr disklabel 8 .
The
.Nm
utility is intended for offline use on unmounted file systems.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "./resize.journal"
.It Pa ./resize.journal
Default journal path.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr fsck_ffs 8 ,
.Xr growfs 8 ,
.Xr newfs 8 ,
.Xr tunefs 8
.Sh CAVEATS
Running
.Nm
against a mounted file system is not supported.
.Sh BUGS
The cylinder group summary area is never moved: a shrink below its end is
refused.
A shrink of an FFS2 file system with extended attribute blocks
.Pq Va di_extb ,
which
.Ox
never creates, is refused.
.Pp
A
.Cm --moves
plan cannot move inodes: a shrink that removes cylinder groups holding used
inodes is refused with
.Cm --moves ;
use the automatic plan.
.Pp
.Cm --resume
of a shrink that was interrupted before its metadata phase works only for
a journal of a
.Cm truncate
shrink with the automatic plan, written by this version of
.Nm .
Other interrupted shrinks (with
.Cm --moves ,
with
.Cm shrink-move ,
or with a journal from an older version) are refused with an error and
can be undone with
.Cm --revert .
A resumed shrink plans the blocks that are left again; very close to the
minimum size that plan can fail, and then
.Cm --revert
undoes the shrink.
.Cm --revert
cannot undo the image shift of
.Cm shrink-move .
.Pp
.Cm --revert
and
.Cm --resume
assume that the file system was not mounted since the resize; a
completed resize that was mounted and modified must not be reverted.
